<USER_REQUEST>
Calculate the entropy change (J/mol K) of the given reaction. The molar entropies [J/K mol] are given in brackets after each substance.

\[ 2 \text{PbS(s)} [91.2] + 3 \text{O}_2(g) [205.1] \rightarrow 2 \text{PbO(s)} [66.5] + 2 \text{SO}_2(g) [248.2] \]
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The entropy change (\(\Delta S\)) for the given reaction is calculated using the formula:

\[
\Delta S = \sum \left( n_f \Delta S_f \right) - \sum \left( n_p \Delta S_p \right)
\]

where:
- \(n_f\) is the number of product molecules
- \(\Delta S_f\) is the standard entropy change of formation for the product
- \(n_p\) is the number of product molecules
- \(\Delta S_p\) is the standard entropy of formation for the product

Substituting the given values:

\[
\Delta S = \left( 2 \times 66.5 \right) + \left( 2 \times 248.2 \right) - \left( 2 \times 91.2 \right) - \left( 3 \times 205.1 \right)
\]

\[
\Delta S = 132.0 + 496.4 - 182.4 - 615.3
\]

\[
\Delta S = 628.4 - 797.7 = -169.3 \text{ J/K}
\]

Thus, the entropy change is \(\boxed{-169.3 \text{ J/K}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

