<start_of_turn>model
","a) **Series System**  
In a series system, the system fails if one or more components fail. For the system to be reliable, all components must be reliable.  
- The reliability function \( R_S(t) \) is the product of the reliability functions of all components:  
  \[
  R_S(t) = R_1(t) \times R_2(t) \times R_3(t) \times R_4 \times R_5 \times R_6
  \]  
  where \( R_i(t) \) is the reliability function of component \( i \).  

b) **Parallel System**  
In a parallel series, the system is reliable if one or more cells are reliable. The system fails only if all components fail.  
- The reliability function \( R_S(t) \) is the complement of the probability that all cells fail:  
  \[
  R_S(t) = 1 - \left(1 - R_1(t)\right) \times \left(1 - R_2(t)\right) \times \left(1 - R_3(t)\right) \times \left(1 - R_4\right) \times \left(1 - R_5\right) \times \left(1 - R_6\right)
  \]  
  where \( R_i(t) \) is the reliability function of component \( i \).  

c) **Combination of Series and Parallel Systems**  
