","To prove that \( U(r, \theta) \) is harmonic inside the circle \( |z| = 1 \), we use the method of contour mapping. The function \( U(r, \theta) \) is defined in polar coordinates as:
\[
U(r, \theta) = \frac{2}{\pi} \tan^{-1} \left( \frac{2r \sin \theta}{1 - r^2} \right), \quad 0 < r < 1, \  0 \leq \theta < 2\pi.
\]
The complex function \( f(z) \) is given by:
\[
f(z) = \frac{2}{r} U(r, \theta) = \frac{2}{r} \frac{2}{\pi} \tan^{-1} \left( \frac{2r \sin \theta}{1 - r^2} \right) = \frac{4}{r\pi} \tan^{-1} \left( \frac{2r \sin \theta}{1 - r^2} \right).
\]
The complex function is \( f(z) = f(r, \theta) \). The complex function is analytic on the unit disk \( |z| < 1 \), so it is analytic and analytic everywhere on the unit disk. Since \( f(z) \) is analytic, its derivative is zero:
\[
\frac{\partial^2}{\partial z^2} f(z) = 0.
\]
