An amplifier has a voltage gain \( A_v = 100 \) without feedback. A fraction \( m_v = 0.1 \) of the output is fed back in opposition to the input (negative feedback). If the gain \( A_v \) falls by 6 dB due to ageing, calculate the percentage change in the closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \).
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
1. **Calculate the initial closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \):**
   The closed-loop voltage gain with negative feedback is given by:
   \[
   A_{vf} = \frac{A_v}{1 + m_v}
   \]
   Substituting the given values:
   \[
   A_{vf} = \frac{100}{1 + 0.1} = \frac{100}{1.1} = 90.9 \text{ dB}
   \]

2. **Calculate the new voltage gain \( A_v' \):**
   The gain decreases by 6 dB, so the new gain is:
   \[
   A_{vf} = 10^{-0.6} \times 100 = 10^{0.6} = 100 \times 10^{-0.6} = 100 \times 0.11 = 11 \text{ dB}
   \]

3. **Calculate the new closed-loop gain \( A_{vf'} \):**
   Using the same formula with the new gain:
   \[
   A_{fv'} = \frac{A_{v}'}{1 + m_v} = \frac{11}{1 + 0.1} = \frac{11}{1.1} = 10 \text{ dB}
   \]

4. **Calculate the percentage change in the closed-loop gain:**
   The percentage change is:
   \[
   \text{Percentage Change} = \left( \frac{A_{vf'} - A_{vf}}{A_{vf}} \right) \times 100 = \left( \frac{10 - 90.9}{90.9} \right) \times 100 = \left( \frac{-80.9}{90.9} \right) \times 100 = -88.8\%
   \]

   **Final Answer:** The percent change in the closed-loop gain is \(\boxed{-88.8\%}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

