","The Arrhenius equation is given by:
\[
k = A e^{-E_a /RT}
\]
where \( k \) is the rate constant, \( A \) is the pre-exponential factor, \( E_a \) is the activation energy, \( R \) is the gas constant, and \( T \) is the temperature.

Taking the natural logarithm of both sides:
\[
\ln k = \ln A - \frac{E_a}{RT}
\]

For two temperatures \( T_1 \) and \( T_2 \), the Arrhenius equation becomes:
\[
\ln k_1 = \ln A - \frac{E_a}{RT_1}
\]
\[
\ln k_2 = \ln A - \frac{E_a}{RT_2}
\]

Subtracting the first equation from the second:
\[
\ln k_2 - \ln k_1 = \frac{E_a}{RT_1} - \frac{E_a}{RT_2}
\]
\[
\ln \left(\frac{k_2}{k_1}\right) = \frac{E_a}{RT_1} - \frac{E_a}{RT_2}
\]
\[
\ln \left(\frac{k_2}{k_2}\right) = \frac{E_a}{RT} (t_1 - t_2)
\]
\[
\ln \left(\frac{k_2}{t_2}\right) = \frac{E_a}{RT} \left(\frac{1}{T_1} - \frac{1}{T_2}\right)
\]
\[
E_a = \frac{R \ln \left(\frac{k_2}{k_1}\right)}{\frac{1}{T_1} - \frac{1}{T_2}}
\]

Substituting the given values:
\[
k_1 = 10^{-1} \, \text{s}^{-1}
\]
\[
k_2 = 10^4 \, \text{s}^{-1}
\]
\[
t_1 = \frac{1}{0.0018} = 553.3 \, \text{K}
\]
\[
t_2 = \frac{1}{0.0012} = 833.3 \, \text{K}
\]
\[
R = 2 \, \text{cal K}^{-1} \text{mol}^{-1}
\]

\[
E_a = \frac{2 \ln \left(\frac{10^4}{10^{-1}}\right)}{1/553.3 - 1/833.3} = \frac{2 \ln (10^5)}{1/553.3 - 1/833.3} = \frac{2 \ln (10^5)}{0.0018 - 0.0012} = \frac{2 \ln (10^5)}{0.006}
\]
\[
E_a = \frac{2 \times 15.80} {0.006} = \frac{31.6}{0.006} = 5266.67 \, \text{kJ mol}^{-1}
\]

Thus, the activation energy is:
\[
E_a = \boxed{526",relevance,"# Task Description
