","To calculate the recoil velocity of the Earth, we use the conservation of momentum. The total momentum before the explosion (or in this case, the explosion of the cannonball) is zero, as both the cannonball and Earth are initially at rest. After the explosion, the cannonball moves with a velocity \( v_{\text{cannonball}} \) and the Earth moves with a recoil velocity \( v_{\text{Earth}} \). By conservation of momentum:

\[
m_{\text{cannonball}} \cdot v_{\text{cannonball}} = -m_{\text{Earth}} \cdot v_{\text{Earth}}
\]

Solving for \( v_{\text{Earth}} \):

\[
v_{\text{Earth}} = -\frac{m_{\text{cannonball}} \cdot v_{\text{cannonball}}}{m_{\text{Earth}}}
\]

Substituting the given values:

\[
v_{\text{Earth}} = -\frac{(1 \text{ kg})(90.0 \text{ m/s})}{5.97 \times 10^{24} \text{ kg}}
\]

\[
v_{\text{Earth}} = -1.52 \times 10^{-23} \text{ m/s}
\]

The negative sign indicates that the Earth moves in the opposite direction of the cannonball. The magnitude of the recoil velocity is:

\[
\boxed{1.52 \times 10^{-23} \text{ m/s}}
\]

This is a very small velocity, indicating that the Earth's recoil is negligible compared to the cannonball's velocity.",relevance,"# Task Description
