","To determine the total entropy increase of the combined system, we analyze the entropy changes of the gas and the water separately.

1. **Entropy change of the gas (\(S_{\text{gas}}\)):**
   The gas cools from 1100°C to 550°C. The heat lost by the gas is:
   \[
   Q_{\text{gas}} = m_{\text{gas}} c_{\text{gas}} \Delta T = m_{\text{gas}} \times 1.005 \times (1100 - 550) = 552.75 m_{\text{gas}} \text{ kJ}
   \]
   The entropy change of the gas is:
   \[
   S_{\text{gas}} = \frac{Q_{\text{gas}}}{T_{\text{gas}}} = \frac{m_{\text{gas}} \times 1.005 \times 552.75}{T_{\text{gas}}} = \frac{552.75 m_{\text{gas}}}{T_{\text{gas}}} \text{ kJ/K}
   \]
   The temperature \(T_{\text{gas}}\) is the average temperature of the gas:
   \[
   T_{\text{gas}} = \frac{1100 + 550}{2} = 332.5 \text{ K}
   \]
   Substituting:
   \[
   S_{\text{gas}} = \frac{552.75 m_{\text{gas}}}{332.5} = 1.66 \times 10^3 m_{\text{gas}} \text{ kJ/K}
   \]

2. **Entropy change of the water (\(S_{\text{water}}\)):**
   The water vaporizes at 220°C. The heat absorbed by the water is:
   \[
   Q_{\text{water}} = m_{\text{water}} L = 1 \times 1858.5 = 1885.5 \text{ kJ}
   \]
   The entropy change of the water is:
   \[
   S_{\text{water}} = \frac{Q_{\text{water}}}{T_{\text{water}}} = \frac{1885.5}{220 + 273} = \frac{1885.5}{493} = 3.85 \text{ kJ/K}
   \]

3. **Total entropy change (\(S_{\text{total}}\)):**
