","To compute the curl of a vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) in a Cartesian coordinate system, we use the formula:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \begin{vmatrix}
\hat{i} & \hat{j} & \hat{k} \\
\frac{\partial}{\partial x} & \frac{\partial}{\partial y} & \frac{\partial}{\partial z} \\
u & v & w
\end{vmatrix}.
\]
Given the vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\), we can write it as:
\[
\mathbf{V}(x, y) = \langle u(x, y), v(x, y), 0 \rangle,
\]
where \(u(x, y)\) and \(v(x, y)\) are scalar functions of \(x\) and \(y\). Substituting these into the curl formula, we get:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \begin{vmatrix}
\hat{i} & \hat{j} & \hat{k} \\

\frac{\partial}{\partial x} & \frac{\partial}{\partial y} & \frac{\partial}{\partial z} \\
u(x, y) & v(x, y) & 0
\end{vvmatrix}.
\]
Expanding the determinant, we get:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \hat{i} \left( \frac{\partial}{\partial y} (0) - \frac{\partial}{\partial y} v(x, y) \right) - \hat{j} \left( \frac{\partial}{\partial x} (0) - \frac{\partial}{\partial x} v(x, y) \right) + \hat{k} \left( \frac{\partial}{\partial x} v(x, y) - \frac{\partial}{\partial y} u(x, y) \right).
\]
Simplifying, we get:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \hat{i} ( 0 - \frac{\partial v}{\partial y} ) - \hat{j} ( 0 - \frac{\partial v}{\partial x} ) + \hat{k} \left( \frac{\partial v}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial u}{\partial y} \right).
\]
Thus, the curl is:
\[
\boxed{\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left( -\frac{\partial v}{\partial y} \right) \hat{i} + \left( \frac{\partial v}{\partial x} \right) \hat{j} + \left( \frac{\partial v}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial u}{\partial y} \right) \hat{k}}.
